export const mechaber = {
  "1#main": "The laws of blisters and lung adhesions. In it are 7 seifim. A lung in which blisters are found — even if they are very large — if they were full of air, or clear water, or moisture stretched like honey and the like, or dry hard moisture even like stone — it is permitted. If putrid moisture or putrid water or murky water is found in them — it is treifah. {Rama: And many permit even murky and putrid (Rosh, Ran, Rashba, Tur, Maharil, and above siman 36 seif 7 on that basis).} And the custom is to permit blisters in every case if they are not adjacent.",
  "2#main": "If an adhesion hangs out emerging from the blister — treifah. {Rama: The same applies to a blister standing in a place of other flaws — even if it would be kosher otherwise, such as on a protrusion or in a place that makes a sound, or the blister itself makes a sound — treifah (found written).}",
  "3#main": "If it has two blisters adjacent to one another — forbidden, even if full of clear water; and if it is one and appears as two, they puncture it on one side — if they pour to one place it is one and kosher; if not, they are two and treifah. This is when there is mugla or water in them; but if they are tinnirim (hard growths) {Rama: The same applies to morsa on the lung — meaning the lung membrane covers it and it is not higher than lung flesh (Mahariu and other acharonim)} — even though adjacent — kosher. {Rama: And they are not called adjacent unless there is not between them the gap of two hairs after inflating the lung (Tosafot and Hagahot Ashiri, and see there) {Taz}: Provided that between them there is lung appearance (Machatzit HaShekel and Bedek HaBayit in the name of Mahara\"k) {Beur HaGra} {Peleti}. Some permit even two adjacent blisters if the water in them is clear, sweet (Rashbam, Rivash, Mordechai, and see there, Mahariu) {Taz}: One may follow custom (it appears the Maharil was precise when he wrote in the slopes) or according to the matter of loss and time of pressure {Baer Hetev}. Bubbles in the slopes of the lung with clear water — their law is like adjacent (darkei emet from the poskim). A blister on a groove and wrinkle — treifah, for it is like two adjacent blisters {Shach} {Kereti} {Baer Hetev} (Machatzit HaShekel). Two blisters opposite one another between two onot — kosher. But a blister opposite tinnir in this manner — treifah, for the tinnir is hard and punctures the blister opposite it (Machatzit HaShekel) {Kereti}: Bubbles and tinnir adjacent to one another — kosher (darkei emet).}",
  "4#main": "A blister found in the slopes of the lung — they practice to forbid if there is no flesh whatsoever surrounding it on every side {Beur HaGra} (and specifically a blister; but tinnir is kosher in the slopes of the lung) (his own view and Mahari and Hagahot Maimoniyot). And slopes means the bottom of the ama or ona, but not the cuts between ona and ona. {Rama: And some say that wherever there is a sharp point it is called slopes of the lung (Mahariu, Semak, and Kol Bo); therefore one should declare treifah even above in the place where the lung adheres to the spine if it is opposite the cut of the onot where the lung is still sharp and called slopes; but opposite the onot — kosher, for there it is not slopes, for there the lung is wide (in examinations). And one may follow custom in this; and if there is no thread of flesh surrounding, they inflate it and feel toward the side of the blister — if through this a thread of flesh surrounds — kosher (Beit Yosef) {Shach} {Taz}: Provided the surrounding flesh has lung appearance {Baer Hetev} (Bedek HaBayit in the name of Mahara\"k) {Shach}: And if there was no flesh appearance surrounding and afterward examiners came and inflated and felt, and through their expertise and strength they inflated well and felt, and made it that there was flesh surrounding — kosher (Beit Yosef siman 39 in the name of responsum of Rivash and an incident with the Rosh).}",
  "5#main": "A blister in the lung cut across to the other side — even though they do not pour to one another — kosher. {Rama: Provided they do not stand in the place of bronchi; but if they stand in the place of bronchi — meaning two fingerbreadths from the slopes in a thin animal and four in a thick one — they practice to declare treifah (Mahariu; and see s.k. 23); and one should not deviate {Taz} {Shach} {Peleti}, and even clear water in the blister (Mahara\"k root 36, darkei emet) — and specifically when they pour to one another {Shach} {Beur HaGra}; but if they do not pour to one another — kosher (Mahariu there) {Shach}, even murky {Baer Hetev} and putrid (darkei emet, and so wrote the poskim). A bubble under the varda in the thin place where there is a bronchus — treifah, for it is recognizable across {Kereti} {Baer Hetev} (Machatzit HaShekel and Mahariu). Tinnir recognizable across — its law is like a blister (old examinations) {Shach}. And see above siman 42 what is called pouring to one another.}",
  "6#main": "A blister in which a puncture is found where the slaughterer's hand did not feel, and he does not know whether it was punctured while alive or after death — treifah.",
  "7#main": "A blister or adhesion found in the windpipe of the heart or anywhere outside the lung — kosher (and see end of siman 46).",
};

